 before they moved to geneva  the when you look at the situation now  libya  when when the divide was going on  it created some sort of regional  realignment  egypt the uae and russia they were  providing support for the  east qatar turkey were providing support  for the  west is it is is the image  is the political landscape still the  same  uh definitely uh uh as far as as uh the  emirates is concerned is no longer  providing the uh support it was  providing after before uh it has been uh  reduced tremendously if not uh stopped  altogether uh it found i guess in the  baby a known quantity the imarat has  known baba since uh he was a high  government official in the qadhafi  regime  and they seem to be comfortable with the  with them  and they are in constant communications  the egyptians don't want elections  because elections mean definitely  that aguero salah is not going to be  back as head of the parliament and  hafter  will will be under great pressure  because he's always claimed he has  always claimed that he will only submit  to an elected  government in libya and they know that a  a new elected government in libya may  not guarantee the survival of after so  the egyptians are adamant about opposing  elections and supporting aguila salah  and after also noticed that  haftar and aguila they were the ones who  opposed the government of uh  of uh the gn the gna the government of  national accord of mr saraj  since its beginning and that government  was born out of the rat agreement  between  the parliament and the  gnc at that time  after again after agreement between the  the the parliament and the uh uh how the  high council of state and other uh  parties in geneva  uh declared three months after the after  the government got its uh mandate from  the parliament that he does not  recognize this government and banned mr  baba from entering the east or the south  let me go to that's why uh  so that's why stephanie williams in her  opening speech in cairo said we tried  everything we tried transitional  government we tried power sharing  agreements we have to go to elections  because all other solutions and she also  said we need consensus sylvia do you  think that with the cairo talks  that could be an opportunity to  reconcile some of the differences  between the key architects particularly  the turks the egyptians the emiratis the  qataris about how to move forward  well uh the cairo talks have been uh uh  crafted in a way to kind of go uh in  parallel to the the process that started  in berlin  in uh january 2020 and there was at the  berlin two conference one year ago um  and i don't see why the the characters  should  succeed in bringing you know the  original players or the backers of the  different factions in libya to  i mean common terms  and  particularly because as it was said the  cairo doesn't have any interest in  leveling the playing field and allowing  libyans to choose freely  who is going to basically  rule them so i don't really trust  this process to have any meaningful  results it is a good  opportunity for the time being to keep  the channels of communication open but i  don't think it's gonna solve the major  problems and uh this is an example of  the fact that the regional players are  back in uh in the field in controlling  the process because the international  community and they are in particular  has kind of lost control of the  situation mustafa this must be a very  delicate moment for the libyan people  there  they're looking at the situation the  politicians are jostling for power and  bigger say and at the same time the  they're concerned that this could be  conducive to civil war once again in the  country  well however unlikely the possibility of  another civil war is always there and  libya's always a bigger kind and present  risk and we have to remember that  the risk has increased since  the  the representative of mr after on the  five plus five military commission which  brought in the cease-fire in 2020  uh has already suspended  its participation the whole group five  of them and they not just that they  recommended to mr hafter to suspend  consider the ceasefire  suspended and  even asked them to take certain measures  to make sure that it is  not in place anymore and of course they  as usual they accused the other side the  government jnu uh and in tripoli uh  violating the terms of the ceasefire and  they they are partly right in this  accusation because mr babe when bashar  was voted in  last february uh took certain measures  that really violated the ceasefire  but but but  not in terms of i say  violence erupting again  yes  thank you thank you very much indeed  mustafa ferturi sylvia colombo salah  bakusha really appreciate your insight  and thank you two for watching you can  see the program again anytime by  visiting our website aljazeera.com for  further discussion go to our facebook  page that's facebook.com forward slash  aj inside story you can also join the  conversation on twitter our handle is at  aj inside story from hashimoto and the  entire team here in doha bye for now  [Music]
 

